9dc22c2b-f121-4692-bb4d-1035567a8325,a9cf55df-8748-49fc-890d-b58d123a143f,identifier,"doi; conflicts=yid,dc.identifier.doi,dc.identifier.scopusid,dc.identifier.uri,dc.identifier.wosid",identifier_conflict,"[""9dc22c2b-f121-4692-bb4d-1035567a8325"", ""123456789/2"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Lichev L.||Mitsche D.||Warnke L."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""2023-03-08T20:09:36Z||2023-03-08T20:05:21Z"", """", ""2023-03-08T20:09:36Z||2023-03-08T20:05:21Z"", """", """", """", """", ""2022||2023"", """", """", """", """", """", ""© 2022 Wiley Periodicals LLC.The modularity of a graph is a parameter that measures its community structure; the higher its value (between 0 and 1), the more clustered the graph is. In this paper we show that the modularity of a random 3-regular graph is at least 0.667026 asymptotically almost surely (a.a.s.), thereby proving a conjecture of McDiarmid and Skerman. We also improve the a.a.s. upper bound given therein to 0.789998. For a uniformly chosen graph (Formula presented.) over a given bounded degree sequence with average degree (Formula presented.) and with (Formula presented.) many connected components, we distinguish two regimes with respect to the existence of a giant component. In the subcritical regime, we compute the second term of the modularity. In the supercritical regime, we prove that there is (Formula presented.), for which the modularity is a.a.s. at least (Formula presented.) where (Formula presented.) is the asymptotically almost sure limit of (Formula presented.).||© 2023 The Authors. Random Structures & Algorithms published by Wiley Periodicals LLC.The clique chromatic number of a graph is the smallest number of colors in a vertex coloring so that no maximal clique is monochromatic. In 2016 McDiarmid, Mitsche and Pra?at noted that around (Formula presented.) the clique chromatic number of the random graph (Formula presented.) changes by (Formula presented.) when we increase the edge-probability (Formula presented.) by (Formula presented.), but left the details of this surprising “jump” phenomenon as an open problem. We settle this problem, that is, resolve the nature of this polynomial “jump” of the clique chromatic number of the random graph (Formula presented.) around edge-probability (Formula presented.). Our proof uses a mix of approximation and concentration arguments, which enables us to (i) go beyond Janson's inequality used in previous work and (ii) determine the clique chromatic number of (Formula presented.) up to logarithmic factors for any edge-probability (Formula presented.)."", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""NSF||Fondecyt"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""802"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""10.1002/rsa.21080"", """", """", """", ""1098-2418"", """", """", """", """", """", ""1042-9832"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS_ID:85125047972"", """", ""http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1002/(ISSN)1098-2418||https://doi.org/10.1002/rsa.21080||https://sipa.uc.cl/handle/123456789/377066"", """", ""WOS:000759608900001"", """", ""Instituto de Ingeniería Matemática y Computacional; Mitsche Dieter Wilhelm; S/I; 1207024"", """", ""4"", """", """", ""en"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""802"", """", ""754"", """", """", """", """", """", ""John Wiley and Sons Ltd"", """", """", """", """", ""Random Structures and Algorithms"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Random Structures and Algorithms"", """", """", """", """", """", ""acceso abierto"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""modularity||random graphs with given degree sequence||random regular graphs||clique chromatic number||critical window||phase transition||random graph"", """", """"