87c6821b-daea-4e0a-94f2-7df4b12880d1,a2b76aae-832d-46b2-80c4-221bb9402743,identifier,"doi; conflicts=yid,dc.identifier.uri",identifier_conflict,"[""87c6821b-daea-4e0a-94f2-7df4b12880d1"", ""123456789/2"", """", """", """", """", ""dfo||carga"", """", """", """", """", ""Villar-Salvador, Pedro||Andivia, Enrique||Mariotti, Barbara||Oliet, Juan A.||Puértolas, Jaime||Cocozza, Claudia||Ivetić, Vladan||Tsakaldimi, Marianthi||Montagnoli, Antonio||Cvjetković, Branislav||Dumroese, R. Kasten||Fløistad, Inger S.||Arellano Ogaz, Eduardo Carlos||Devetaković, Jovana||Diez, Julio J.||Li, Guolei||Maltoni, Alberto||Ovalle, Juan F.||Salbitano, Fabio||Tognetti, Roberto||Witzell, Johanna||Villar-Salvador P.||Andivia E.||Mariotti B.||Oliet J.A.||Puertolas J.||Cocozza C.||Ivetic V.||Tsakaldimi M.||Montagnoli A.||Cvjetkovic B.||Dumroese R.K.||Floistad I.S.||Arellano E.||Devetakovic J.||Diez J.J.||Li G.||Maltoni A.||Ovalle J.F.||Salbitano F.||Tognetti R.||Ventura M.||Vilagrosa A.||Witzell J."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-05-04T12:54:02Z||2026-05-31T12:36:36Z"", """", ""2026-05-04T12:54:02Z||2026-05-31T12:36:36Z"", """", """", """", """", ""2026"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Seedling functional attributes (i.e., morphological and physiological traits driving water, carbon, and nutrient economy, as well as stress resistance and resilience) influence the early performance of forest plantings. Nursery environment and cultivation practices interact with species ecology to shape these attributes and potential outplanting success. Although extensive literature exists on these topics, studies that have quantitatively synthesized findings to generalize plant quality and nursery cultivation theory are almost nonexistent. This chapter quantitatively reviews the effects of (1) seedling size and shoot-to-root mass ratio (S/R) and (2) several nursery cultivation practices on outplanting survival and growth. Examined practices include stock type (container vs. bareroot (BR)), drought and blackout hardening, container properties in oaks and pines, and growing media alternatives to peat. For this, we developed different databases compiling information at a global scale from scientific and technical literature in different languages. Seedling size significantly enhances outplanting survival when comparing seedlings of the same age, while S/R does not. Stocktype and species-specific leaf area (SLA), a trait related to resource acquisition capacity and stress resistance in plants, modulate the effect of morphology on survival, particularly on arid sites. In dry climates, large seedlings have a survival advantage over small seedlings in low SLA species (i.e., slow growth and high stress resistance plants) cultivated in containers, if water stress is mitigated through intensive soil preparation, and using 1-year-old seedlings. When stock types differ in survival, container plants usually outperform BR stock, especially if soil preparation is performed, and under dry and high weed competition conditions. Drought hardening improves survival, especially in shrubs, but can negatively affect survival in drought-sensitive species on low aridity sites. Blackout reduces field growth, particularly in drought-tolerant species. The effect of container characteristics on survival differs between oaks and pines: for oaks, reducing plant spacing in small to moderate containers (<400 mL) is preferable to maximize survival, while for pines, increasing container depth is more effective. Peat alternatives, including manure, organic waste, and rice hulls, improved survival, whereas sludge performed poorly. Rice was most effective on humid sites, while manure and organic waste showed consistent benefits across climates. Our findings highlight the potential for peat alternatives, with effectiveness depending on local ecological and economic conditions. Based on these results, we provide recommendations to match seedlings’ size and S/R to species' SLA, stocktype, soil preparation intensity, and aridity of the planting site, along with cultivation guidelines for producing such a variety of seedlings.||© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.Seedling functional attributes (i.e., morphological and physiological traits driving water, carbon, and nutrient economy, as well as stress resistance and resilience) influence the early performance of forest plantings. Nursery environment and cultivation practices interact with species ecology to shape these attributes and potential outplanting success. Although extensive literature exists on these topics, studies that have quantitatively synthesized findings to generalize plant quality and nursery cultivation theory are almost nonexistent. This chapter quantitatively reviews the effects of (1) seedling size and shoot-to-root mass ratio (S/R) and (2) several nursery cultivation practices on outplanting survival and growth. Examined practices include stock type (container vs. bareroot (BR)), drought and blackout hardening, container properties in oaks and pines, and growing media alternatives to peat. For this, we developed different databases compiling information at a global scale from scientific and technical literature in different languages. Seedling size significantly enhances outplanting survival when comparing seedlings of the same age, while S/R does not. Stocktype and species-specific leaf area (SLA), a trait related to resource acquisition capacity and stress resistance in plants, modulate the effect of morphology on survival, particularly on arid sites. In dry climates, large seedlings have a survival advantage over small seedlings in low SLA species (i.e., slow growth and high stress resistance plants) cultivated in containers, if water stress is mitigated through intensive soil preparation, and using 1-year-old seedlings. When stock types differ in survival, container plants usually outperform BR stock, especially if soil preparation is performed, and under dry and high weed competition conditions. Drought hardening improves survival, especially in shrubs, but can negatively affect survival in drought-sensitive species on low aridity sites. Blackout reduces field growth, particularly in drought-tolerant species. The effect of container characteristics on survival differs between oaks and pines: for oaks, reducing plant spacing in small to moderate containers (<400mL) is preferable to maximize survival, while for pines, increasing container depth is more effective. Peat alternatives, including manure, organic waste, and rice hulls, improved survival, whereas sludge performed poorly. Rice was most effective on humid sites, while manure and organic waste showed consistent benefits across climates. Our findings highlight the potential for peat alternatives, with effectiveness depending on local ecological and economic conditions. Based on these results, we provide recommendations to match seedlings’ size and S/R to species' SLA, stocktype, soil preparation intensity, and aridity of the planting site, along with cultivation guidelines for producing such a variety of seedlings."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""ORCID||SCOPUS"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""10.1016/b978-0-443-34086-4.00005-0"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""978-0-443-34086-4"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS_ID:105039741055"", """", ""https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-443-34086-4.00005-0||https://sipa.uc.cl/handle/123456789/422094"", """", """", """", ""Facultad de Agronomía e Ingenieria Forestal; Arellano Ogaz Eduardo Carlos; 0000-0002-2000-3386; 93756"", """", """", """", """", ""en||eng"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Contenido parcial"", """", ""191"", """", ""131"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Elsevier"", """", """", """", """", ""Guidelines for Climate Adaptive Forest Restoration and Reforestation Projects"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Guidelines for Climate Adaptive Forest Restoration and Reforestation Projects"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Acceso restringido||acceso abierto"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""600"", """", """", """", """", ""09 Industry, innovation and infrastructure"", """", ""09 Industria, innovación e infraestructura"", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Alternative growing media||Aridity||Container structure||Functional traits||Planting practices||Seedling hardening||Seedling size||Shoot-to-root ratio||Stocktypes||Survival"", """", """", """", """", ""Chapter 5 - Quality matters. A quantitative review on the effect of seedling morphology and nursery practices on the outplanting performance of forest plantings||Quality matters. A quantitative review on the effect of seedling morphology and nursery practices on the outplanting performance of forest plantings"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Chapter 5 - Quality matters. A quantitative review on the effect of seedling morphology and nursery practices on the outplanting performance of forest plantings||Quality matters. A quantitative review on the effect of seedling morphology and nursery practices on the outplanting performance of forest plantings"", ""capítulo de libro||libro"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""93756"", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-05-06;93756;1"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Scopus"", """", """", """", """", """", ""ORCID;2026-05-04||SCOPUS;2026-05-31"", """", """", ""Validado||Sin validar"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Validado"", """", ""2026-05-06;93756;1"", """"]","[""a2b76aae-832d-46b2-80c4-221bb9402743"", ""123456789/2"", """", """", """", """", ""carga"", """", """", """", """", ""Villar-Salvador P.||Andivia E.||Mariotti B.||Oliet J.A.||Puertolas J.||Cocozza C.||Ivetic V.||Tsakaldimi M.||Montagnoli A.||Cvjetkovic B.||Dumroese R.K.||Floistad I.S.||Arellano E.||Devetakovic J.||Diez J.J.||Li G.||Maltoni A.||Ovalle J.F.||Salbitano F.||Tognetti R.||Ventura M.||Vilagrosa A.||Witzell J."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-05-31T12:36:36Z"", """", ""2026-05-31T12:36:36Z"", """", """", """", """", ""2026"", """", """", """", """", """", ""© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.Seedling functional attributes (i.e., morphological and physiological traits driving water, carbon, and nutrient economy, as well as stress resistance and resilience) influence the early performance of forest plantings. Nursery environment and cultivation practices interact with species ecology to shape these attributes and potential outplanting success. Although extensive literature exists on these topics, studies that have quantitatively synthesized findings to generalize plant quality and nursery cultivation theory are almost nonexistent. This chapter quantitatively reviews the effects of (1) seedling size and shoot-to-root mass ratio (S/R) and (2) several nursery cultivation practices on outplanting survival and growth. Examined practices include stock type (container vs. bareroot (BR)), drought and blackout hardening, container properties in oaks and pines, and growing media alternatives to peat. For this, we developed different databases compiling information at a global scale from scientific and technical literature in different languages. Seedling size significantly enhances outplanting survival when comparing seedlings of the same age, while S/R does not. Stocktype and species-specific leaf area (SLA), a trait related to resource acquisition capacity and stress resistance in plants, modulate the effect of morphology on survival, particularly on arid sites. In dry climates, large seedlings have a survival advantage over small seedlings in low SLA species (i.e., slow growth and high stress resistance plants) cultivated in containers, if water stress is mitigated through intensive soil preparation, and using 1-year-old seedlings. When stock types differ in survival, container plants usually outperform BR stock, especially if soil preparation is performed, and under dry and high weed competition conditions. Drought hardening improves survival, especially in shrubs, but can negatively affect survival in drought-sensitive species on low aridity sites. Blackout reduces field growth, particularly in drought-tolerant species. The effect of container characteristics on survival differs between oaks and pines: for oaks, reducing plant spacing in small to moderate containers (<400mL) is preferable to maximize survival, while for pines, increasing container depth is more effective. Peat alternatives, including manure, organic waste, and rice hulls, improved survival, whereas sludge performed poorly. Rice was most effective on humid sites, while manure and organic waste showed consistent benefits across climates. Our findings highlight the potential for peat alternatives, with effectiveness depending on local ecological and economic conditions. Based on these results, we provide recommendations to match seedlings’ size and S/R to species' SLA, stocktype, soil preparation intensity, and aridity of the planting site, along with cultivation guidelines for producing such a variety of seedlings."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""10.1016/B978-0-443-34086-4.00005-0"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS_ID:105039741055"", """", ""http://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-443-34086-4.00005-0||https://sipa.uc.cl/handle/123456789/422628"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""eng"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""191"", """", ""131"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Elsevier"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Guidelines for Climate Adaptive Forest Restoration and Reforestation Projects"", """", """", """", """", """", ""acceso abierto"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Alternative growing media||Aridity||Container structure||Functional traits||Planting practices||Seedling hardening||Seedling size||Shoot-to-root ratio||Stocktypes||Survival"", """", """", """", """", ""Quality matters. A quantitative review on the effect of seedling morphology and nursery practices on the outplanting performance of forest plantings"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Quality matters. A quantitative review on the effect of seedling morphology and nursery practices on the outplanting performance of forest plantings"", ""libro"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Scopus"", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS;2026-05-31"", """", """", ""Sin validar"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Validado"", """", """", """"]"
75324723-53e2-46af-bee9-b47107c35ea5,119a7310-171c-4c1e-a475-91f6f807367a,identifier,"doi; conflicts=yid,dc.identifier.uri",identifier_conflict,"[""75324723-53e2-46af-bee9-b47107c35ea5"", ""123456789/2"", """", """", """", """", ""carga"", """", """", """", """", ""Algeria, Isabel Zapata||Zapata Alegria Isabel"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-03-20T12:12:36Z||2026-05-31T12:37:44Z"", """", ""2026-03-20T12:12:36Z||2026-05-31T12:37:44Z"", """", """", """", """", ""2026"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Within the framework of the neoliberalism that is characteristic of advanced capitalism, urban development leads to structural socio-spatial inequalities. This scenario puts pressure on democracies by testing the public function of states in terms of the implementation of urban planning processes aimed at achieving more equitable cities (Fainstein, 2010). In this context, the question arises as to how to advance towards urban justice in planned cities that nevertheless experience persistent inequalities in the current political and economic scenario. This article proposes an analytical framework for examining planning practice, constructed based on four categories for the analysis of socio-spatial inequality derived from theory: building unjust urban space, the position of subjects discriminated against by the space they inhabit, the inequitable distribution of public assets, and exclusionary political representation. The conclusions provide guidelines to be incorporated into the creation of territorial planning instruments, with the aim of guiding public action towards greater levels of equity and urban justice.||© 2026 (Universitat Autonoma de Barcelona). All rights reserved.Planned cities and processes of socio-spatial inequality in Latin America: How to advance urban justice? Within the framework of the neoliberalism that is characteristic of advanced capitalism, urban development leads to structural socio-spatial inequalities. This scenario puts pressure on democracies by testing the public function of states in terms of the implementation of urban planning processes aimed at achieving more equitable cities (Fainstein, 2010). In this context, the question arises as to how to advance towards urban justice in planned cities that nevertheless experience persistent inequalities in the current political and economic scenario. This article proposes an analytical framework for examining planning practice, constructed based on four categories for the analysis of socio-spatial inequality derived from theory: building unjust urban space, the position of subjects discriminated against by the space they inhabit, the inequitable distribution of public assets, and exclusionary political representation. The conclusions provide guidelines to be incorporated into the creation of territorial planning instruments, with the aim of guiding public action towards greater levels of equity and urban justice."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""WOS||SCOPUS"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""10.5565/rev/dag.1249"", """", """", """", ""2014-4512"", """", """", """", """", """", ""0212-1573"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS_ID:105038229741"", """", ""https://doi.org/10.5565/rev/dag.1249||https://sipa.uc.cl/handle/123456789/419097"", """", ""WOS:001696445600001"", """", ""Instituto de Estudios Urbanos y Territoriales; Zapata Alegría, María Isabel; S/I; 1186510||Instituto de Estudios Urbanos y Territoriales; Zapata Alegria Isabel; S/I; 1186510"", """", ""1"", """", """", ""es||spa"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""25"", """", ""5"", """", """", """", """", """", ""UNIV AUTONOMA BARCELONA||Universitat Autonoma de Barcelona"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""DOCUMENTS D ANALISI GEOGRAFICA"", """", """", """", """", """", ""acceso abierto"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""socio-spatial inequalities||urban planning||just cities||ciudad justa||ciutat justa||desigualdades socioespaciales||desigualtats socioespacials||inégalités socio-spatiales||planificació urbana||planificación urbana||urbanisme||ville juste"", """", """", """", """", ""Planned cities and processes of socio-spatial inequality in Latin America: How to advance urban justice?||Ciudades planificadas y sus procesos de desigualdad socioespacial en Latinoamérica: ¿cómo avanzar en justicia urbana?"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Planned cities and processes of socio-spatial inequality in Latin America: How to advance urban justice?||Ciudades planificadas y sus procesos de desigualdad socioespacial en Latinoamérica: ¿cómo avanzar en justicia urbana?"", ""artículo"", """", """", """", """", """", ""72"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""1186510"", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-06-30;1186510;1||2026-07-29;1186510;1"", """", """", """", """", """", ""WOS_ID: PMV-1818-2026"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""WOS||Scopus"", """", """", """", """", """", ""WOS;2026-03-19||SCOPUS;2026-05-31"", """", """", ""Sin validar"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Validado"", """", ""2026-06-30;1186510;1||2026-07-29;1186510;1"", """"]","[""119a7310-171c-4c1e-a475-91f6f807367a"", ""123456789/2"", """", """", """", """", ""carga"", """", """", """", """", ""Zapata Alegria Isabel"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-05-31T12:37:44Z"", """", ""2026-05-31T12:37:44Z"", """", """", """", """", ""2026"", """", """", """", """", """", ""© 2026 (Universitat Autonoma de Barcelona). All rights reserved.Planned cities and processes of socio-spatial inequality in Latin America: How to advance urban justice? Within the framework of the neoliberalism that is characteristic of advanced capitalism, urban development leads to structural socio-spatial inequalities. This scenario puts pressure on democracies by testing the public function of states in terms of the implementation of urban planning processes aimed at achieving more equitable cities (Fainstein, 2010). In this context, the question arises as to how to advance towards urban justice in planned cities that nevertheless experience persistent inequalities in the current political and economic scenario. This article proposes an analytical framework for examining planning practice, constructed based on four categories for the analysis of socio-spatial inequality derived from theory: building unjust urban space, the position of subjects discriminated against by the space they inhabit, the inequitable distribution of public assets, and exclusionary political representation. The conclusions provide guidelines to be incorporated into the creation of territorial planning instruments, with the aim of guiding public action towards greater levels of equity and urban justice."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""10.5565/REV/DAG.1249"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""0212-1573"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS_ID:105038229741"", """", ""http://doi.org/10.5565/REV/DAG.1249||https://sipa.uc.cl/handle/123456789/422653"", """", """", """", ""Instituto de Estudios Urbanos y Territoriales; Zapata Alegria Isabel; S/I; 1186510"", """", """", """", """", ""spa"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""25"", """", ""5"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Universitat Autonoma de Barcelona"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Documents d'Analisi Geografica"", """", """", """", """", """", ""acceso abierto"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""ciudad justa||ciutat justa||desigualdades socioespaciales||desigualtats socioespacials||inégalités socio-spatiales||just cities||planificació urbana||planificación urbana||socio-spatial inequalities||urban planning||urbanisme||ville juste"", """", """", """", """", ""Ciudades planificadas y sus procesos de desigualdad socioespacial en Latinoamérica: ¿cómo avanzar en justicia urbana?"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Ciudades planificadas y sus procesos de desigualdad socioespacial en Latinoamérica: ¿cómo avanzar en justicia urbana?"", ""artículo"", """", """", """", """", """", ""72"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""1186510"", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-07-29;1186510;1"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Scopus"", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS;2026-05-31"", """", """", ""Sin validar"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Validado"", """", ""2026-07-29;1186510;1"", """"]"
4779b9b0-474e-473c-9c98-263bcb1625f5,82508278-0934-4f4a-a0fa-45d0aa04929d,identifier,"doi; conflicts=yid,dc.identifier.uri",identifier_conflict,"[""4779b9b0-474e-473c-9c98-263bcb1625f5"", ""123456789/2"", ""2010"", """", """", """", ""yvc||carga"", """", """", """", """", ""Aguilar-Gálvez, Ana||García-Ríos, Diego||Quispe, Teresa||Ames, Cristopher||Figueroa-Merma, Andrés||Chirinos, Rosana||Pedreschi Plasencia, Franco Wilfredo||Pedreschi, Romina||Campos, David||Aguilar-Galvez A.||Garcia-Rios D.||Quispe T.||Ames C.||Figueroa-Merma A.||Chirinos R.||Pedreschi F.||Pedreschi R.||Campos D."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""2026-06-08T12:53:18Z||2026-06-21T12:31:09Z"", """", ""2026-06-08T12:53:18Z||2026-06-21T12:31:09Z"", """", """", """", """", ""2026"", """", """", """", """", """", ""© 2026 by the authors.In this study, the formation of Maillard reaction products (MRPs), namely acrylamide (AA) and fluorescent advanced glycation end products (f-AGEs), was evaluated in four quinoa and four cañihua flours subjected to heating at 185 °C. The influence of endogenous phenolic compounds and the addition of tara hydrolyzed gallotannins on MRPs formation was investigated. Varieties with higher quercetin and rutin contents showed lower AA and f-AGEs formation. AA formation kinetics differed between species, increasing with heating time in quinoa, whilst decreasing after 20 min in cañihua. Maximum AA levels ranged from 18.6 to 87.0 μg/kg, remaining below the EU benchmark value for non-wheat-based bread (300 μg/kg). Compared with the Asn–Glc control system, flour systems reduced AA formation by approximately 18.7 to 60.3%, while tara hydrolyzed extract further enhanced inhibition, reaching reductions above 72.7 to 96.2%. Similar trends were observed for f-AGEs, with mitigation levels up to 54.1 to 77.4%. Cañihua varieties showed higher AA inhibition capacity than quinoa, likely associated with differences in phenolic composition and antioxidant capacity. These findings demonstrate the potential of Andean pseudocereals and tara-derived polyphenols as natural strategies to mitigate thermally induced contaminants in processed foods."", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""6/11/2026"", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""ORCID||SCOPUS"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""10.3390/foods15112010"", """", """", """", ""2304-8158"", """", """", """", """", """", ""2304-8158"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""SCOPUS_ID:105041547810"", """", ""https://doi.org/10.3390/foods15112010||https://sipa.uc.cl/handle/123456789/422853"", """", """", """", ""Escuela de Ingeniería; Pedreschi Plasencia Franco Wilfredo; 0000-0003-0599-8466; 18770"", """", """", """", """", ""en||eng"", """", """", """", """", """", ""contenido completo"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Foods"", """", """", """", ""CC BY Atribución/Reconocimiento Internacional 4.0"", """", ""acceso abierto"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""620"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""Andean pseudocereals||Thermal processing||Maillard reaction||Antioxidants||Acrylamide||Fluorescent AGEs"", """", """", """", """", ""Role of Endogenous and Exogenous Phenolic Compounds on the Formation of Acrylamide and Fluorescent Advanced Glycation End Products in Heated Quinoa and Cañihua Flours"", """", """", """", """", """", ""Role of Endogenous and Exogenous Phenolic Compounds on the Formation of Acrylamide and Fluorescent Advanced Glycation End Products in Heated Quinoa and Cañihua Flours"", ""artículo"", """", """", """", """", """", ""15"", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", """", ""18770"", """", """", """", "